<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img class="phero-img" src="/assets/img/photos/calculator-hands.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-hands-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator-hands.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Claim glossary</span></nav><p class="kicker kicker-light">Reference</p><h1>Property Insurance Claim Glossary for Connecticut</h1><p class="phero-lede">41 terms you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English. Type in the box to filter.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “depreciation”, “umpire” or “ice dam”" autocomplete="off"><p class="hint" data-gloss-count>41 terms</p></div><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></div><dl class="glossary"><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-A">Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay ACV first and the rest after repairs.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for extra costs of living elsewhere while your home is being repaired, also called loss of use.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of a covered loss. This is the only question an appraisal panel decides.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision that sets the amount of loss. It is binding once signed by any two of the three panel members.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy condition that lets either party demand appraisal when they disagree about the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Appraiser</dt><dd>The person each side names to the panel. Policies usually require a competent and disinterested appraiser.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-B">Bad faith</dt><dd>A general term for an insurer’s unreasonable handling of a claim. In Connecticut, related claims may involve CUIPA, CUTPA or common law. A legal question for an attorney.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-C">Coinsurance</dt><dd>A commercial policy clause that can reduce payment if a property was insured for less than a required percentage of its value.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Connecticut FAIR Plan</dt><dd>Connecticut’s insurer of last resort for property owners who can’t obtain coverage in the voluntary market.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Connecticut Insurance Department</dt><dd>The state agency that regulates insurers, licenses adjusters and handles consumer complaints.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>Dwelling coverage on a homeowners policy, used to calculate percentage deductibles.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other structures coverage for detached garages, sheds, fences and similar structures.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>Personal property, or contents, coverage.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>CUIPA</dt><dd>The Connecticut Unfair Insurance Practices Act, which lists unfair claim settlement practices.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-D">Debris removal</dt><dd>Coverage for removing debris of covered property after a loss. Tree debris often has its own small limit.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of your policy listing coverages, limits and deductibles.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value for age and wear, subtracted from replacement cost to get actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Disinterested</dt><dd>Having no financial stake in the outcome. Appraisal clauses usually require disinterested appraisers.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-H">Hurricane deductible</dt><dd>A deductible, often a percentage of Coverage A, that applies to losses from a hurricane when the policy’s trigger is met.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-I">Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the eaves that traps meltwater, which can back up under shingles and leak inside.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-L">Line item</dt><dd>A single entry on an estimate, such as a quantity of shingles or a square footage of drywall, with its unit price.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-M">Matching</dt><dd>The issue of whether replacement materials reasonably match existing materials, which can affect how far repairs extend.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping or drying. Usually a policy duty.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-N">Named storm</dt><dd>A tropical system named by the National Hurricane Center. Some deductibles are triggered by named storms.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-O">Ordinance or law</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs to meet current building codes during repairs, subject to its own limit.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charge for coordinating multiple trades, commonly 10% plus 10%.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-P">Pitch</dt><dd>A roof’s slope expressed as rise over 12 inches of run, such as 9/12.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, which the insurer may request within a set time.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Public adjuster</dt><dd>A licensed professional who represents the policyholder in presenting and negotiating the whole claim for a fee.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-R">Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation held back from the first payment and paid once repairs are completed under a replacement cost policy.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without deducting depreciation.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-S">Scope</dt><dd>The list of repairs needed to restore the property. Many disputes are about scope rather than price.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Square</dt><dd>100 square feet of roof surface.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Standard fire policy</dt><dd>A policy form set out in Connecticut statute, on which many property policies, including their appraisal provisions, are based.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Steep charge</dt><dd>An added labor charge for working on steep roofs, commonly applied from about 7/12 pitch.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Storm surge</dt><dd>Seawater pushed ashore by a storm. Usually excluded from homeowners policies and covered by flood insurance.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>The policy’s deadline for filing a lawsuit against the insurer.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request to add items or costs to an existing estimate, supported by documentation.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-U">Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of the appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers can’t agree on.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt>Uniform appearance</dt><dd>The goal of repairs that blend with undamaged areas. Related to matching disputes.</dd></div><div class="g-row"><dt id="g-X">Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers and contractors to price repairs by line item.</dd></div></dl><p class="gloss-none" hidden>No terms match that search.</p></article>
<aside class="side"><div class="side-sticky"><div class="side-box side-call"><h2 class="side-t">Talk it through first</h2><p>A free, no-pressure conversation. We’ll say plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="side-phone" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> +1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-coral btn-block" href="/contact-ct-appraiser/">Send claim details</a></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-t">Resources</h2><ul class="side-list"><li><a href="/ct-appraisal-process/">Appraisal process guide<svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/ct-appraisal-faq/">Appraisal FAQ<svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/ct-claim-glossary/" aria-current="page">Claim glossary<svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/ct-claim-insights/">Claim insights<svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li></ul></div></div></aside></d